\cancel{2}\\cdot a & ≡\\cancel{2}\\cdot b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,\\cancel{2}\\cdot 4) \\\\ a & ≡b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415049 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152096 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3822141e-3cea-5c34-b925-a522c2273335" :knowledge-point/title "Combining the Division Properties"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "411fcf4c-0dfe-5eb4-b277-2957160dccc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414950} {:answer/id #uuid "74b71bad-1c7b-5ec7-82dd-47d6ce2c7ca6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414967} {:answer/id #uuid "81d02ebf-0e9e-55cb-a573-4ae072ffecb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414972} {:answer/id #uuid "dfb5e76a-e012-5b9d-add1-ff7fc168f39c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415005} {:answer/id #uuid "ffca300c-7c35-5dd6-9629-dda4cf9c21da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415016}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dfb5e76a-e012-5b9d-add1-ff7fc168f39c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415005} :answer-field/id #uuid "c26a95a5-c027-5bfd-8dc6-5ac33606e4cd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415037}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ca808cf3-f807-5d95-a81f-b5460b52df5b" :question/math-academy-id "q-213392" :question/problem "Given that $3a≡3b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,9),$ which of the following statements **must** be true?\n\nI. $a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3)$\n\nII. $a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,9)$\n\nIII. $4a≡4b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3)$" :question/worked-solution "The division properties of modular arithmetic state the following:\n\n- For $k\\ne 0,$ we have\n\n$$\nka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,kn)\\,⟺\\,a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\n- For $\\text{gcd}(k,n)=1,$ we have \n\n$$\nka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. Applying the first property, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3a & ≡3b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,9) \\\\ 3\\cdot a & ≡3\\cdot b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,3\\cdot 3) \\\\ \\cancel{3}\\cdot a & ≡\\cancel{3}\\cdot b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,\\cancel{3}\\cdot 3) \\\\ a & ≡b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. By statement I and the second property, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3)\\,⟺\\,4a≡4b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415051 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152094 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bca70f6c-d087-5b8e-aff2-581adca9ed1f" :knowledge-point/title "Using the First Division Property"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ad3b1aa-93e7-5b73-a4b4-ec7fb6b5d2f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414948} {:answer/id #uuid "9b4bf61e-6b83-5157-b763-8513937c1774" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414983} {:answer/id #uuid "be01c4c9-9b6c-5fb4-b2ec-7c85edac2cfd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414996} {:answer/id #uuid "e0a31f1f-fd54-58eb-b1f3-5e9c47ac9da1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415006} {:answer/id #uuid "e7f12825-c2b7-5186-8218-7c940c5283cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415007}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e7f12825-c2b7-5186-8218-7c940c5283cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415007} :answer-field/id #uuid "5139e167-eff8-5fb2-95ba-ff706a30c921" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415026}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d23e3ed0-5575-5880-b9bf-7ff1adfe9936" :question/math-academy-id "q-103168" :question/problem "Given that $3a≡3b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,12),$ which of the following statements **must** be true?\n\nI. $a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3)$\n\nII. $a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,4)$\n\nIII. $a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6)$" :question/worked-solution "The division property of modular arithmetic states that for $k\\ne 0,$ we have\n\n$$\nka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,kn)\\,⟺\\,a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nWe're asked to consider the following statement:\n\n$$\n3a≡3b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,12)\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\text{gcd}(3,12)=3.$\n\nApplying the division property, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3a & ≡3b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,12) \\\\ 3\\cdot a & ≡3\\cdot b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,3\\cdot 4) \\\\ \\cancel{3}\\cdot a & ≡\\cancel{3}\\cdot b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,\\cancel{3}\\cdot 4) \\\\ a & ≡b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415061 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124485 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "486cc512-416b-5eac-8964-1a87d533de72" :knowledge-point/title "Determining if a Given Set Is Linearly Independent Given Information on Its Elements"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1688faeb-85ba-5d68-81e7-78a4a337f227" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415055} {:answer/id #uuid "7b430210-150a-5113-bb9e-9ceedc527951" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415056} {:answer/id #uuid "888ec5b1-c2ea-5a99-8c7a-f1ea5fd2dbc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415057} {:answer/id #uuid "a66c38ea-8581-5edc-9742-8946b8e3e270" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415058} {:answer/id #uuid "f381c62f-1e63-5006-ba9d-a84a80c6800c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415059}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b430210-150a-5113-bb9e-9ceedc527951" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415056} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0e50222-5322-5dc6-95c0-87aed25c41a3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415060}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0c07b7ca-2c8b-5a74-a857-c6a369e5fb28" :question/math-academy-id "q-35580" :question/problem "Consider a vector space $V$ and vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}\\in V.$ Given that ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}}$ is linearly dependent and ${{v}_{1},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}}$ is linearly independent, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}+{x}_{4}{v}_{4}=0$ has only zero solution ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}={x}_{4}=0$\n- ${v}_{2}$ is a linear combination of ${v}_{1}$, ${v}_{3}$ and ${v}_{4}$\n- ${v}_{4}$ is a linear combination of ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{3}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the given options in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}}$ is a linearly dependent set, we obtain that\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}+{x}_{4}{v}_{4}=0\n$$\n\nhas a non-zero solution.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, solving equation ${x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}+{x}_{4}{v}_{4}=0$ for ${v}_{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=-\\frac{{x}_{1}}{{x}_{2}}{v}_{1}-\\frac{{x}_{3}}{{x}_{2}}{v}_{3}-\\frac{{x}_{4}}{{x}_{2}}{v}_{4}.\n$$\n\nMoreover, ${x}_{2}$ can't be zero because then\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}+{x}_{4}{v}_{4}=0\n$$\n\nwill have a non-zero solution and that's a contradiction as ${{v}_{1},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}}$ is linearly independent.\nHence, ${v}_{2}$ is a linear combination of ${v}_{1}$, ${v}_{3}$ and ${v}_{4}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the set ${{v}_{1},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}}$ is linearly independent set, the equation \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{3}+{x}_{3}{v}_{4}=0\n$$\n\nhas no non-zero solution. Hence, ${v}_{4}$ is not a linear combination of ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{3}$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415272 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172054 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "72015667-33a9-53e2-a0c4-09d8ce916b54" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Vector Fields That Are Independent of Path"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15a9cbc7-f277-56ad-a34d-b1e67b9ccb79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415200} {:answer/id #uuid "4199a3f5-5c75-59ad-ae31-6dbe20749db3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415211} {:answer/id #uuid "7254ab02-972a-5fc3-af1e-ece583b386ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415224} {:answer/id #uuid "95a9fd1b-4296-5d3a-ad4e-a9e88dba336a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415235} {:answer/id #uuid "bed907e5-7397-570c-82dd-cbe5f3babe74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415243}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7254ab02-972a-5fc3-af1e-ece583b386ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415224} :answer-field/id #uuid "65c1d218-3b72-5245-bad5-dd08cb553d78" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415261}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0027cfb5-d932-549f-8cc6-973642895e8d" :question/math-academy-id "q-125743" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=⟨{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y,\\,{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y⟩$ and the points $O(0,0)$ and $A(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ in the $xy$-plane. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $F$ is conservative.\n\nII. ${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=e,$ where ${C}_{1}$ is the path along the line segment $\\overset{―}{OA},$ traversed from $O$ to $A.$\n\nIII. ${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=2e-1,$ where ${C}_{2}$ is the path along the curve $y=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\sqrt{x},$ traversed from $O$ to $A.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nWith that in mind, we consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ with domain $D={R}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives exist and are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative.\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $F$ is conservative, there exists a function $f$ such that $∇f=F.$ Reconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\n$$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2})-f(0,0) \\\\  & ={e}^{1}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-{e}^{0}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡0 \\\\  & =e\\cdot 1-0 \\\\  & =e.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $F$ is conservative and ${C}_{2}$ has the same initial and terminal points as ${C}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=e.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415275 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172054 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "72015667-33a9-53e2-a0c4-09d8ce916b54" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Vector Fields That Are Independent of Path"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d8bc88c-65ec-5ed0-861b-da4a28dd2e6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415207} {:answer/id #uuid "5d32c371-2553-5a69-82d8-90b81f6734fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415218} {:answer/id #uuid "6b142c8d-3eea-55c4-9289-bbf7b0018f9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415221} {:answer/id #uuid "878097e9-8b52-5d48-a250-9feb84ddeb46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415227} {:answer/id #uuid "8d3bdd36-68bf-5e48-b9c0-a73c00b3435d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415232}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "878097e9-8b52-5d48-a250-9feb84ddeb46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415227} :answer-field/id #uuid "d6f00848-516a-51d1-8994-e5331367e330" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415270}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "41c787c2-8fe3-51ae-aef3-bfbcd04677ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-125720" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=⟨y,\\,x⟩$ and the points $O(0,0)$ and $A(\\sqrt{2},2)$ in the $xy$-plane. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $F$ is conservative.\n\nII. ${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=2\\sqrt{2},$ where ${C}_{1}$ is the path along the line segment $\\overset{―}{OA},$ traversed from $O$ to $A.$\n\nIII. ${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=-\\sqrt{2},$ where ${C}_{2}$ is the path along the curve $y={x}^{2},$ traversed from $O$ to $A.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nWith that in mind, we consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ with domain $D={R}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives exist and are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative.\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $F$ is conservative, there exists a function $f$ such that $∇f=F.$ Reconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf=xy.\n$$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(\\sqrt{2},2)-f(0,0) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot 2-0\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $F$ is conservative and ${C}_{2}$ has the same initial and terminal points as ${C}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=2\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415281 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172054 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "72015667-33a9-53e2-a0c4-09d8ce916b54" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Vector Fields That Are Independent of Path"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "036e6f03-ee36-5c5f-b814-7fbdad1c16c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415195} {:answer/id #uuid "789ac7c1-845d-586f-9c96-a1af50adc74b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415225} {:answer/id #uuid "b090af4c-99bd-5025-98a2-d50859d47fe1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415240} {:answer/id #uuid "deac1bbc-f701-5baa-b26c-08f5a0fcebf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415253} {:answer/id #uuid "f1e8d299-71bd-546e-9566-9e1178edccba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415257}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "789ac7c1-845d-586f-9c96-a1af50adc74b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415225} :answer-field/id #uuid "b08ccadb-6ec6-59d9-86d6-45aa650a0703" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415266}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ef46b0f0-6f4c-53dd-b0f1-5e1b301fe037" :question/math-academy-id "q-125736" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=⟨{e}^{y},\\,x{e}^{y}⟩$ and the points $A(1,0)$ and $B(3,\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)$ in the $xy$-plane. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $F$ is conservative.\n\nII. ${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=8,$ where ${C}_{1}$ is the path along the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x),$ traversed from $A$ to $B.$\n\nIII. ${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=3,$ where ${C}_{2}$ is the path along the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ traversed from $A$ to $B.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nWith that in mind, we consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ with domain $D={R}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={e}^{y}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives exist and are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative.\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $F$ is conservative, there exists a function $f$ such that $∇f=F.$ Reconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf=x{e}^{y}.\n$$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(3,\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)-f(1,0) \\\\  & =3\\cdot {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}-1\\cdot {e}^{0} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 3-1 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $F$ is conservative and ${C}_{2}$ has the same initial and terminal points as ${C}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=8.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415479 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133913 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "abaeea03-6a2a-5d37-a2af-a9ea109175ad" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Reasons Why a Determinant Equals Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d00532a-c12b-50b9-ad0b-b9f1fa474794" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415449} {:answer/id #uuid "44173bde-0fab-57de-b1b3-22d94262de8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415452} {:answer/id #uuid "6b9c1118-9a19-54c3-9683-cf1248dd342b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415456} {:answer/id #uuid "7e862f84-0fe3-5f4e-8829-b54fb65649ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415457} {:answer/id #uuid "e9cd19d7-7290-5199-aa60-b1bc0b2d73b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415464}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e9cd19d7-7290-5199-aa60-b1bc0b2d73b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415464} :answer-field/id #uuid "e80361a1-690f-54bf-a979-055f8bec64f8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415473}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f8a11ae3-b740-5975-a251-a4796d69b742" :question/math-academy-id "q-43307" :question/problem "Let $A$ be a $3\\times 3$ matrix such that $|A|=0.$ Which of the following statements must be true?\n\n- $A$ has a zero column\n- $A$ has two proportional rows\n- One of the rows/columns of $A$ is a linear combination of other rows/columns of $A$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider our statements in turn.\n\n- Statements I and II are false. For example, consider the matrix \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nExpending across the $1$st column, we have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A| & =1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}|+1|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(3-2)+(1-2) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nbut $A$ has neither a zero column nor two proportional rows.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, we can make use of the following fact:\n\n$|A|=0$ *if and only if one of the rows/columns of $A$ is a linear combination of other rows/columns of $A.$*\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416481 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184197 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8063bc9c-a10d-562b-93b1-508b4b26a674" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Singular Values of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c278b14-28c2-57de-b60a-2a720876d406" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416447} {:answer/id #uuid "5b060c6a-de3b-54b0-bfda-579bbdcbb6d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416451} {:answer/id #uuid "5ec6c063-a755-58f5-8636-29649c9e3395" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416452} {:answer/id #uuid "7c2cf85b-73a0-5874-97a9-cfa33e64d6b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416455} {:answer/id #uuid "e71d59cc-b53d-52ee-9d28-5a2450433b8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416469}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5ec6c063-a755-58f5-8636-29649c9e3395" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416452} :answer-field/id #uuid "389a199b-2132-5200-92d1-2261a44348a8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416476}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5650e0d2-585c-5516-892d-5eacef061cb2" :question/math-academy-id "q-95266" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- Any $2\\times 3$ matrix $A$ has at most $2$ distinct singular values.\n- The matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]$ has one negative singular value.\n- Any $5\\times 3$ matrix $A$ has at most $3$ distinct singular values." :question/worked-solution "The singular values of the matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $A$ is a $2\\times 3$ matrix and ${A}^{T}$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix, ${A}^{T}\\,A$ must be a $3\\times 3$ matrix: \n\n$$\n{{A}^{T}}_{3\\times 2}\\times {A}_{2\\times 3}={{A}^{T}\\,A}_{3\\times 3}\n$$\n\nHence, $A$ has at most $3$ distinct singular values.\n\n- Statement II is false. All singular values of any matrix are non-negative. So, $A$ can't have a negative singular value.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $A$ is a $5\\times 3$ matrix and ${A}^{T}$ is a $3\\times 5$ matrix, ${A}^{T}\\,A$ must be a $3\\times 3$ matrix: \n\n$$\n{{A}^{T}}_{3\\times 5}\\times {A}_{5\\times 3}={{A}^{T}\\,A}_{3\\times 3}\n$$\n\nHence, $A$ has at most $3$ distinct singular values.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416485 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184197 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8063bc9c-a10d-562b-93b1-508b4b26a674" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Singular Values of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d306b2f-e020-5c0b-8b36-318a141afa1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416448} {:answer/id #uuid "884aa6f5-9cee-5d6c-a6d2-1e3e9fda993d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416458} {:answer/id #uuid "c41c10ba-d192-5a1a-9031-b8c0dab1e30f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416465} {:answer/id #uuid "d11ab6aa-4e8f-5b94-8bb5-66fd5a8105bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416467} {:answer/id #uuid "f351ce40-cb01-5b7d-a5fb-a7f513b6a235" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416473}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4d306b2f-e020-5c0b-8b36-318a141afa1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416448} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8cda2f1-5552-5357-82cc-f1ab61cac36e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416480}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e69dd032-aa5c-5891-a7fc-97b84703e88a" :question/math-academy-id "q-95261" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- Any $4\\times 3$ matrix $A$ has at most $3$ distinct singular values.\n- The singular values of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{aligned}]$ are non-negative.\n- Any $3\\times 4$ matrix $A$ has at most $3$ distinct singular values." :question/worked-solution "The singular values of the matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $A$ is a $4\\times 3$ matrix and ${A}^{T}$ is a $3\\times 4$ matrix, ${A}^{T}\\,A$ must be a $3\\times 3$ matrix: \n\n$$\n{{A}^{T}}_{3\\times 4}\\times {A}_{4\\times 3}={{A}^{T}\\,A}_{3\\times 3}\n$$\n\nHence, $A$ has at most $3$ distinct singular values.\n\n- Statement II is true. All singular values of any matrix are non-negative.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $A$ is a $3\\times 4$ matrix and ${A}^{T}$ is a $4\\times 3$ matrix, ${A}^{T}\\,A$ must be a $4\\times 4$ matrix: \n\n$$\n{{A}^{T}}_{4\\times 3}\\times {A}_{3\\times 4}={{A}^{T}\\,A}_{4\\times 4}\n$$\n\nHence, $A$ has at most $4$ distinct singular values.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416486 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184197 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8063bc9c-a10d-562b-93b1-508b4b26a674" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Singular Values of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4727adce-5afb-5a6e-baec-86299311d447" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416444} {:answer/id #uuid "4ab592cf-fe9a-5d31-b42c-37ad02aab55a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416446} {:answer/id #uuid "862c188a-6765-5375-b045-c13862731467" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416456} {:answer/id #uuid "9d9dee5f-cd09-5188-9a3c-cde7f4e68a37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416461} {:answer/id #uuid "f25d7873-4bce-5acd-bc01-3466707919a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416472}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f25d7873-4bce-5acd-bc01-3466707919a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416472} :answer-field/id #uuid "d97b8052-bb1d-524c-878a-c0e3b532b8c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416479}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fef86daf-091a-52fa-bcf0-1d2b609bb348" :question/math-academy-id "q-95281" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- Any $6\\times 3$ matrix $A$ has at most $3$ distinct singular values.\n- The matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}]$ has one negative singular value.\n- Any $4\\times 5$ matrix $A$ has at most $4$ distinct singular values." :question/worked-solution "The singular values of the matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since $A$ is a $6\\times 3$ matrix and ${A}^{T}$ is a $3\\times 6$ matrix, ${A}^{T}\\,A$ must be a $3\\times 3$ matrix: \n\n$$\n{{A}^{T}}_{3\\times 6}\\times {A}_{6\\times 3}={{A}^{T}\\,A}_{3\\times 3}\n$$\n\nHence, $A$ has at most $3$ distinct singular values.\n\n- Statement II is false. All singular values of any matrix are non-negative. So, $A$ can't have a negative singular value.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $A$ is a $4\\times 5$ matrix and ${A}^{T}$ is a $5\\times 4$ matrix, ${A}^{T}\\,A$ must be a $5\\times 5$ matrix: \n\n$$\n{{A}^{T}}_{5\\times 4}\\times {A}_{4\\times 5}={{A}^{T}\\,A}_{5\\times 5}\n$$\n\nHence, $A$ has at most $5$ distinct singular values.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417219 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119926 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "19c33c51-bf11-5a81-a0fd-8381d8e0b9d7" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Line Is a Subspace of Two-Dimensional Space"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44a4e1dc-a2d7-5163-8db3-bd5e766783de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417213} {:answer/id #uuid "5175130c-a0e7-5993-877f-e784135b6082" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417214} {:answer/id #uuid "7d69583b-f76a-5611-8cb8-4022da72fc78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417215} {:answer/id #uuid "99e8273a-2cc4-5606-ade8-2988cc10652d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417216} {:answer/id #uuid "a149c6f5-ef3c-51ad-997e-db08bfab777c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417217}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5175130c-a0e7-5993-877f-e784135b6082" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417214} :answer-field/id #uuid "7336d919-b96c-5024-ae89-e9caaf4e41d0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417218}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "feee3179-e7d1-55ab-bcac-f334cca10495" :question/math-academy-id "q-156620" :question/problem "Let $L$ be the line with equation $4x=2y$ in ${R}^{2}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $L$ contains the zero-vector\n- $L$ is closed under vector addition\n- $L$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $L$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $L$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ 2x\\end{aligned}],\\,x\\in R.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of the zero-vector satisfy the equation $4x=2y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x & =2y \\\\ 4(0) & =2(0) \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 2a\\end{aligned}]\\in L$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ 2b\\end{aligned}]\\in L.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 2a\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ 2b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}a+b \\\\ 2a+2b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}a+b \\\\ 2(a+b)\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $4x=2y.$ Therefore, $a+b\\in L.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 2a\\end{aligned}]\\in L$ and $k\\in R.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k\\cdot a & =k\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 2a\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}ka \\\\ k(2a)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}ka \\\\ 2(ka)\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhich satisfies the condition $4x=2y.$ Therefore, $k\\cdot a\\in L.$\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since statements II and III are true, $L$ must be a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417703 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124521 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9d77214-796e-558f-a364-00ae3e25cc13" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "64373e5d-5dd7-5b8c-997e-b1a1d51821a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417686} {:answer/id #uuid "7ed9e009-1eed-587e-99c2-a5c90a12f34a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417688} {:answer/id #uuid "bd086813-aae0-5b88-80fb-38d4e70fa634" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417692} {:answer/id #uuid "f5946256-8005-5d78-9fdf-0e9f112f6155" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417697} {:answer/id #uuid "fd2228ec-39b9-5ba7-96a0-e30dc0202e32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417699}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bd086813-aae0-5b88-80fb-38d4e70fa634" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417692} :answer-field/id #uuid "2afbf87d-1c06-55f7-9a1b-f8b6764e8f1d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417701}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0464e7a7-ce22-55cd-90ec-4b6a71a12079" :question/math-academy-id "q-38893" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true given that $T:{R}^{5}\\to {R}^{5}$ is a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T,$ and $T(x)=v$ has no solution? \n\n- $T$ is invertible\n- $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}$\n- $\\text{nullity}(T)=0$" :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{5}$ into ${R}^{5},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{5} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=5 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $T(x)=v$ has no solution, we have that $v\\notin \\text{Im}(T),$ hence $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{5}.$ According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{5},$ the transformation $T$ and the matrix $T$ are not invertible.\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $T$ is not invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that  $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $T$ is not invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}]}
